y": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 45, "source_tag": "Hexaware PGET PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 20, "question_text": "Which SQL clause is required to filter records based on the result of an aggregate function such as COUNT() or AVG()?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "sql", "option_a": "WHERE", "option_b": "HAVING", "option_c": "GROUP BY", "option_d": "ORDER BY", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "WHERE filters individual rows prior to grouping, whereas HAVING filters grouped rows after aggregate computations have taken place.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Hexaware PGET PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 8, "question_text": "Fill in the blank with the appropriate word: 'The executive committee has decided to _____ the policy review meeting until next Tuesday.'", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "defer", "option_b": "differ", "option_c": "defy", "option_d": "deter", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "'Defer' means to postpone or put off to a later time. 'Differ' means disagree, 'defy' means disobey, and 'deter' means discourage.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Hexaware PGET PYQ"}]}, {"code": "PERSISTENT_MOCK_PACK", "title": "Persistent Systems Tech Mock Pack", "description": "Advanced quantitative reasoning, core DBMS/OS fundamentals, and algorithmic coding challenges curated from Persistent Systems tests.", "icon": "bi bi-terminal-fill", "coin_cost": 250, "company_name": "Persistent", "questions": [{"subtopic_id": 2, "question_text": "If the retail price of sugar rises by 25%, by what exact percentage must a household reduce its consumption so that the total monthly expenditure on sugar remains unchanged?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "15%", "option_b": "20%", "option_c": "25%", "option_d": "16.67%", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Formula: Reduction = [r / (100 + r)] * 100. For r = 25: (25 / 125) * 100 = 1/5 * 100 = 20%.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 35, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 2, "question_text": "An item is marked 40% above its cost price and subsequently sold at a discount of 10% on the marked price. What is the net profit percentage realized on the transaction?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "24%", "option_b": "26%", "option_c": "30%", "option_d": "32%", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Let CP = 100. Marked Price = 140. Discount = 10% of 140 = 14. Selling Price = 140 - 14 = 126. Profit = 126 - 100 = 26%.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 40, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 1, "question_text": "A train traveling at a constant speed of 45 km/h takes exactly 12 seconds to completely cross a telegraph pole. What is the length of the train in meters?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "120 meters", "option_b": "140 meters", "option_c": "150 meters", "option_d": "180 meters", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "Convert speed: 45 * (5/18) = 12.5 m/s. Length = Speed * Time = 12.5 m/s * 12 s = 150 meters.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 35, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 12, "question_text": "If 12 software developers can complete a project in 16 days, how many days will 8 software developers take to complete the exact same project at the same productivity rate?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "20 days", "option_b": "24 days", "option_c": "28 days", "option_d": "32 days", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Total person-days = 12 * 16 = 192 days. With 8 developers: 192 / 8 = 24 days.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 24, "question_text": "In how many distinct ways can all the letters of the word 'PERSIST' be arranged?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "2520", "option_b": "5040", "option_c": "1260", "option_d": "720", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "'PERSIST' has 7 letters: P, E, R, S, I, S, T. Notice 'S' appears twice. Total distinct permutations = 7! / 2! = 5040 / 2 = 2520.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 50, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 21, "question_text": "In Python, which list of elements is generated when sorting the array [2, 5, 2, 6, -1, 9, 9, 2, -1] primarily by element frequency descending and secondarily by initial index appearance ascending?", "code_snippet": "arr = [2, 5, 2, 6, -1, 9, 9, 2, -1]\n# Frequency: 2 appears 3 times; -1 and 9 appear 2 times;\n# 5 and 6 appear 1 time.", "language": "python", "option_a": "[2, 2, 2, -1, -1, 9, 9, 5, 6]", "option_b": "[2, 2, 2, 9, 9, -1, -1, 5, 6]", "option_c": "[2, 2, 2, 5, 6, -1, -1, 9, 9]", "option_d": "[9, 9, -1, -1, 2, 2, 2, 5, 6]", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "Frequency breakdown: '2' has freq 3. Both '-1' (first seen at index 4) and '9' (first seen at index 5) have freq 2. Since -1 appeared before 9, -1 precedes 9. Frequencies of 5 and 6 are 1 (5 at idx 1, 6 at idx 3). Result: [2, 2, 2, -1, -1, 9, 9, 5, 6].", "difficulty": "hard", "est_time_seconds": 75, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 21, "question_text": "What is the maximum non-adjacent subset sum for the array [2, 7, 9, 3, 1] using dynamic programming (House Robber formulation)?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "python", "option_a": "11", "option_b": "12", "option_c": "13", "option_d": "14", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], dp[i-2] + nums[i]). Array: [2, 7, 9, 3, 1]. dp[0]=2, dp[1]=7, dp[2]=max(7, 2+9)=11, dp[3]=max(11, 7+3)=11, dp[4]=max(11, 11+1)=12 (taking elements 2 + 9 + 1 = 12).", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 60, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 21, "question_text": "What is the tightest time complexity to locate the k-th node from the end of a singly linked list with N nodes in a single traversal pass using two pointers?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "O(1)", "option_b": "O(k)", "option_c": "O(N)", "option_d": "O(N log N)", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "By placing a fast pointer k nodes ahead of a slow pointer and incrementing both simultaneously until the fast pointer reaches the end, the list is traversed in exactly one pass: O(N) time.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 35, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 20, "question_text": "In Relational Database Management Systems (RDBMS), what characterizes a 'Weak Entity'?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "sql", "option_a": "It cannot be uniquely identified by its own attributes alone and requires an identifying relationship with a strong entity", "option_b": "It has no candidate keys and cannot contain numeric data", "option_c": "It is an unindexed table that cannot participate in joins", "option_d": "It is an entity with only one attribute", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "A weak entity lacks a primary key of its own; its identity depends on a foreign key from a parent strong entity combined with its partial key (discriminator).", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 40, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 14, "question_text": "What is the primary architectural difference between a Mutex and a Semaphore in operating system synchronization?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "A Mutex has an ownership property and can only be released by the locking thread, whereas a Semaphore can be signaled by any thread", "option_b": "A Mutex operates only on hardware interrupts while Semaphores operate on CPU registers", "option_c": "A Semaphore is strictly binary while a Mutex supports integer counters", "option_d": "A Mutex causes busy waiting while Semaphores never block", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "A Mutex is an ownership lock (only the acquiring thread can release it). A Semaphore is a signaling mechanism without ownership semantics, allowing any thread to call post()/signal().", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 45, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 21, "question_text": "What is the worst-case time complexity of searching for an element in an unbalanced Binary Search Tree (BST) containing N nodes?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "O(log N)", "option_b": "O(N)", "option_c": "O(N log N)", "option_d": "O(1)", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "When a BST is skewed (degenerate), it behaves identically to a singly linked list, resulting in worst-case search time of O(N).", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 14, "question_text": "Which OSI Reference Model layer is responsible for end-to-end process-to-process reliable communication, segmenting, and flow control?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "Network Layer", "option_b": "Transport Layer", "option_c": "Session Layer", "option_d": "Data Link Layer", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "The Transport Layer (Layer 4) provides process-to-process delivery, segmentation, flow control, and error recovery (e.g. TCP).", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 15, "question_text": "Why should a base class that contains virtual member functions always declare a virtual destructor in C++?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "cpp", "option_a": "To allow private members to be inherited by derived classes", "option_b": "To ensure that derived class destructors are properly invoked when deleting derived objects via base pointers", "option_c": "To prevent the compiler from generating default constructors", "option_d": "To force static allocation of base class instances", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Without a virtual destructor, deleting a derived object through a Base* pointer triggers undefined behavior and leads to resource/memory leaks because only the base destructor executes.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 45, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 24, "question_text": "A container contains 40 liters of pure milk. From this container, 4 liters of milk are drawn out and replaced with water. This replacement procedure is repeated once more. How many liters of pure milk remain in the container?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "32.0 liters", "option_b": "32.4 liters", "option_c": "34.2 liters", "option_d": "30.8 liters", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Formula: Remaining pure liquid = Initial * (1 - x/V)^n. Here, Initial = 40, x = 4, V = 40, n = 2. Remaining milk = 40 * (1 - 4/40)^2 = 40 * (0.9)^2 = 40 * 0.81 = 32.4 liters.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 60, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 6, "question_text": "Find the missing term in the sequence: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30, 42, ?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "52", "option_b": "54", "option_c": "56", "option_d": "60", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "Pattern is n * (n + 1): 1*2=2, 2*3=6, 3*4=12, 4*5=20, 5*6=30, 6*7=42, 7*8=56 (or consecutive differences +4, +6, +8, +10, +12, +14). 42 + 14 = 56.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Persistent Placement PYQ"}]}, {"code": "VIRTUSA_MOCK_PACK", "title": "Virtusa NeuralHack & Placement Pack", "description": "Core technical MCQs, C pointers, nested pseudo code loops, and quantitative aptitude from Virtusa campus drives.", "icon": "bi bi-code-square", "coin_cost": 250, "company_name": "Virtusa", "questions": [{"subtopic_id": 1, "question_text": "A passenger train running at a speed of 60 km/h crosses a signal pole in 9 seconds. What is the length of the train in meters?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "120 meters", "option_b": "150 meters", "option_c": "180 meters", "option_d": "200 meters", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Speed in m/s = 60 * (5/18) = 50/3 m/s. Length = Speed * Time = (50/3) * 9 = 50 * 3 = 150 meters.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 35, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 2, "question_text": "The cost price of 20 items is identical to the selling price of 'x' items. If the vendor achieves a profit of 25%, what is the value of 'x'?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "15", "option_b": "16", "option_c": "18", "option_d": "25", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Let CP of 1 item = 1 => CP of 20 items = 20. SP of x items = 20 => SP of 1 item = 20/x. Profit = 25% => SP = 1.25 * CP => 20/x = 1.25 => x = 20 / 1.25 = 16.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 45, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 24, "question_text": "What is the difference between Compound Interest and Simple Interest on a principal of ₹5,000 for 2 years at an annual rate of 10%?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "₹25", "option_b": "₹50", "option_c": "₹75", "option_d": "₹100", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "For 2 years, Difference = P * (R / 100)^2 = 5000 * (10 / 100)^2 = 5000 * 0.01 = ₹50.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 35, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 3, "question_text": "If A : B = 2 : 3 and B : C = 4 : 5, find the combined ratio A : B : C in lowest terms.", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "8 : 12 : 15", "option_b": "6 : 10 : 15", "option_c": "4 : 6 : 10", "option_d": "2 : 4 : 5", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "Multiply A:B by 4 -> 8:12. Multiply B:C by 3 -> 12:15. Combined ratio A:B:C = 8:12:15.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 14, "question_text": "What will be printed upon execution of the following nested loop pseudo code snippet?", "code_snippet": "Integer i, j, val\nSet val = 0\nFor i = 1 to 3\n    For j = 1 to i\n        val = val + i + j\n    End For\nEnd For\nPrint val", "language": "c", "option_a": "18", "option_b": "20", "option_c": "25", "option_d": "30", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "i=1, j=1: val = 0 + 1 + 1 = 2. i=2, j=1: val = 2 + 2 + 1 = 5. i=2, j=2: val = 5 + 2 + 2 = 9. i=3, j=1: val = 9 + 3 + 1 = 13. i=3, j=2: val = 13 + 3 + 2 = 18. i=3, j=3: val = 18 + 3 + 3 = 25.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 60, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 14, "question_text": "What will be the output of the following C program with pointer referencing?", "code_snippet": "#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int i = 3;\n    int *j = &i;\n    int **k = &j;\n    printf(\"%d\", **k);\n    return 0;\n}", "language": "c", "option_a": "3", "option_b": "Address of i", "option_c": "Address of j", "option_d": "Garbage value", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "'k' points to 'j', which points to 'i'. Double dereferencing **k yields the integer value stored in 'i', which is 3.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 20, "question_text": "Which SQL JOIN returns all matching rows from both tables, plus all unmatched rows from the left table and all unmatched rows from the right table with NULL fill?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "sql", "option_a": "LEFT OUTER JOIN", "option_b": "RIGHT OUTER JOIN", "option_c": "FULL OUTER JOIN", "option_d": "INNER JOIN", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "A FULL OUTER JOIN combines the results of both LEFT and RIGHT outer joins, showing all records from both tables and filling in NULLs where matches do not exist.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 16, "question_text": "Which Object-Oriented principle binds data and the methods that manipulate that data together into a single class entity while hiding internal representation?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "java", "option_a": "Inheritance", "option_b": "Encapsulation", "option_c": "Polymorphism", "option_d": "Interface segregation", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Encapsulation wraps variables and methods inside classes and restricts direct outside access to internal state using access specifiers like private.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 21, "question_text": "Which core data structure is used natively in Breadth-First Search (BFS) graph traversal to process neighbor vertices level-by-level?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "Stack", "option_b": "Queue", "option_c": "Max-Heap", "option_d": "Binary Tree", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Breadth-First Search requires FIFO (First-In, First-Out) queuing to explore nodes in order of their distance from the source.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 6, "question_text": "Find the next number in the multiplication series: 7, 14, 42, 168, 840, ?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "4200", "option_b": "5040", "option_c": "5880", "option_d": "6720", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Pattern multiplies by successive integers: 7 * 2 = 14; 14 * 3 = 42; 42 * 4 = 168; 168 * 5 = 840; 840 * 6 = 5040.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 35, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 10, "question_text": "If 'CLOUD' is coded as 'DMPVE' in a standard forward-shift cipher, how is 'RAIN' coded in that same system?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "SBJO", "option_b": "SCKP", "option_c": "RBIO", "option_d": "TBJP", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "Each letter is shifted forward by 1: C->D, L->M, O->P, U->V, D->E. Therefore: R->S, A->B, I->J, N->O => SBJO.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 13, "question_text": "What does the idiomatic expression 'Bite the bullet' signify?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "To face an inevitable, difficult situation with bravery and fortitude", "option_b": "To make a rash and dangerous investment", "option_c": "To violently confront a team colleague", "option_d": "To withdraw from a competition at the last minute", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "'Bite the bullet' means to endure a painful or undesirable situation that is seen as unavoidable.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 16, "question_text": "In Java, can a static method directly access non-static instance fields of the same class without instantiating an object?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "java", "option_a": "Yes, static methods have full access to all instance variables", "option_b": "No, static methods belong to the class and have no implicit 'this' reference to instance variables", "option_c": "Yes, but only if the variable is declared protected", "option_d": "Yes, provided the method is public", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Static methods are resolved at class load time and have no object context ('this'), so accessing instance fields directly produces a compilation error.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 12, "question_text": "A can finish a task alone in 12 days, while B can complete the same task alone in 24 days. Working together, how many days will they take to complete it?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "6 days", "option_b": "8 days", "option_c": "9 days", "option_d": "10 days", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Combined rate = 1/12 + 1/24 = (2 + 1)/24 = 3/24 = 1/8. Working together, they require 8 days.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 14, "question_text": "What is the output of this C code snippet with integer division?", "code_snippet": "#include <stdio.h>\nint main() {\n    int a = 7, b = 2;\n    float c = a / b;\n    printf(\"%.2f\", c);\n    return 0;\n}", "language": "c", "option_a": "3.50", "option_b": "3.00", "option_c": "3.5", "option_d": "4.00", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Both 'a' and 'b' are integers, so 'a / b' performs integer truncation yielding 3 before being assigned to the float variable 'c'. Hence, c = 3.00.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 40, "source_tag": "Virtusa Placement PYQ"}]}, {"code": "ORACLE_MOCK_PACK", "title": "Oracle Server Technology Placement Pack", "description": "High-caliber questions on Oracle SQL, transactions, triggers, C++ OOP polymorphism, and speed aptitude from Oracle hiring.", "icon": "bi bi-database-fill-gear", "coin_cost": 250, "company_name": "Oracle", "questions": [{"subtopic_id": 6, "question_text": "Find the missing term in the sequence: 32, 49, 83, 151, 287, 559, ?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "1050", "option_b": "1103", "option_c": "1120", "option_d": "1150", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Differences between consecutive terms double each time: 49 - 32 = 17; 83 - 49 = 34; 151 - 83 = 68; 287 - 151 = 136; 559 - 287 = 272. Next difference is 272 * 2 = 544. Next term = 559 + 544 = 1103.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 55, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 2, "question_text": "The market price of sugar is increased by 25%. By what percentage should a household reduce its consumption so that its total expenditure remains unchanged?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "15%", "option_b": "20%", "option_c": "25%", "option_d": "30%", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Percentage decrease = [r / (100 + r)] * 100 = (25 / 125) * 100 = 20%.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 1, "question_text": "Convert an automobile speed of 90 km/h into meters per second (m/s).", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "20 m/s", "option_b": "25 m/s", "option_c": "30 m/s", "option_d": "35 m/s", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Multiply by 5/18: 90 * (5/18) = 5 * 5 = 25 m/s.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 20, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 20, "question_text": "What is the primary difference in storage mechanism between the CHAR and VARCHAR2 data types in an Oracle Database?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "sql", "option_a": "CHAR pads shorter strings with trailing blank spaces to match declared length, while VARCHAR2 stores only the exact characters without blank padding", "option_b": "CHAR supports Unicode while VARCHAR2 supports only ASCII", "option_c": "VARCHAR2 has a maximum size of 255 bytes while CHAR supports 2GB", "option_d": "CHAR is stored in row while VARCHAR2 is always stored as out-of-line LOB", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "CHAR is fixed-length and blanks-pads shorter inputs up to defined length. VARCHAR2 allocates space only for the actual string length without padding.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 40, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 20, "question_text": "Can Transaction Control Language (TCL) commands such as COMMIT or ROLLBACK be executed directly inside an Oracle database DML row trigger?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "sql", "option_a": "Yes, triggers have full autonomy over transactions", "option_b": "No, because a trigger executes as an integral part of the triggering DML transaction boundary", "option_c": "Yes, but only in AFTER INSERT triggers", "option_d": "Yes, provided the trigger is marked DETERMINISTIC", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "DML triggers execute within the calling transaction's context. Explicit COMMIT or ROLLBACK inside a trigger would violate transaction integrity (unless configured in an AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION pragma).", "difficulty": "hard", "est_time_seconds": 50, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 20, "question_text": "Which SQL construct correctly finds all employees who earn more than the average salary of their specific department?", "code_snippet": "SELECT emp_id, salary, dept_id FROM employees e\nWHERE salary > (\n    SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees\n    WHERE dept_id = e.dept_id\n);", "language": "sql", "option_a": "Correlated Subquery", "option_b": "Recursive CTE", "option_c": "Cross Join", "option_d": "Scalar Literal", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "The inner query references e.dept_id from the outer query, causing it to be evaluated for each outer candidate row: a classic correlated subquery.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 45, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 15, "question_text": "What will be printed when running this C++ program exhibiting virtual function dynamic dispatch?", "code_snippet": "#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\nclass Base {\npublic:\n    virtual void show() { cout << \"Base show\"; }\n};\nclass Derived : public Base {\npublic:\n    void show() { cout << \"Derived show\"; }\n};\nint main() {\n    Base *b = new Derived();\n    b->show();\n    delete b;\n    return 0;\n}", "language": "cpp", "option_a": "Base show", "option_b": "Derived show", "option_c": "Compilation Error", "option_d": "Runtime Exception", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Because 'show()' is declared virtual in Base, C++ uses dynamic vtable dispatch at runtime, calling Derived's overridden version.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 40, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 15, "question_text": "In C++, what does the declaration 'int * const ptr = &val;' designate?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "cpp", "option_a": "A constant pointer to an integer (the address cannot change, but the value pointed to can be modified)", "option_b": "A pointer to a constant integer (the value cannot change, but the address can)", "option_c": "A pointer that can only point to null", "option_d": "A pointer stored in read-only code memory", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "Reading right-to-left: ptr is a const pointer to int. The pointer itself cannot be reassigned to a different memory address, but *ptr can be modified.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 40, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 20, "question_text": "What is the typical time complexity of searching, inserting, and deleting keys in a balanced B-Tree index structure in Oracle?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "sql", "option_a": "O(1)", "option_b": "O(log N)", "option_c": "O(N)", "option_d": "O(N^2)", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Because B-Trees maintain balanced height with large fan-out factors, search, insertion, and deletion scale logarithmically: O(log N).", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 20, "question_text": "Which ACID property guarantees that once a database transaction commits, its modifications are permanently recorded and will survive sudden power loss or crashes?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "sql", "option_a": "Atomicity", "option_b": "Consistency", "option_c": "Isolation", "option_d": "Durability", "correct_option": "D", "explanation": "Durability ensures committed changes are persisted to non-volatile storage (via redo logs / write-ahead logs) and survive catastrophic failures.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 14, "question_text": "Which of the following is NOT one of the four essential Coffman conditions necessary for a system deadlock to manifest?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "Mutual Exclusion", "option_b": "Hold and Wait", "option_c": "Preemptive resource reallocation allowed", "option_d": "Circular Wait", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "Deadlock requires 'No preemption' (resources cannot be forcibly taken away from a process). Allowing preemption actively prevents deadlock.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 40, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 21, "question_text": "What is the tightest time complexity to construct a Binary Max-Heap from an unsorted array of N elements using Floyd's build-heap bottom-up approach?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "O(log N)", "option_b": "O(N)", "option_c": "O(N log N)", "option_d": "O(N^2)", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Floyd's bottom-up build-heap procedure runs in linear O(N) time due to the decaying geometric summation of work across tree levels.", "difficulty": "hard", "est_time_seconds": 50, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 24, "question_text": "Two unbiased six-sided dice are rolled simultaneously. What is the probability that the sum of the numbers appearing on top equals 8?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "5/36", "option_b": "1/6", "option_c": "7/36", "option_d": "1/9", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "Total outcomes = 36. Outcomes summing to 8: (2,6), (3,5), (4,4), (5,3), (6,2) = 5 outcomes. Probability = 5/36.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 35, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 20, "question_text": "In a relational database table definition, what is the constraint rule regarding PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE constraints?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "sql", "option_a": "A table can define only one PRIMARY KEY, but can define multiple UNIQUE constraints", "option_b": "A table can define multiple PRIMARY KEY constraints, but only one UNIQUE constraint", "option_c": "A table cannot combine both PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE constraints", "option_d": "Both constraints must refer to foreign tables", "correct_option": "A", "explanation": "Standard SQL permits exactly one PRIMARY KEY per table, while multiple columns or groups of columns can have separate UNIQUE constraints.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 24, "question_text": "What is the angle between the hour hand and the minute hand of an analog clock at exactly 3:30?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "60 degrees", "option_b": "75 degrees", "option_c": "80 degrees", "option_d": "90 degrees", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Formula: Angle = |30*H - (11/2)*M|. For H=3, M=30: |30*3 - (11/2)*30| = |90 - 165| = |-75| = 75 degrees.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 45, "source_tag": "Oracle Placement PYQ"}]}, {"code": "COFORGE_MOCK_PACK", "title": "Coforge Tech Talent Placement Pack", "description": "Authentic quantitative ratios, series logic, operator precedence pseudo code, and computer science fundamentals from Coforge drives.", "icon": "bi bi-diagram-3-fill", "coin_cost": 250, "company_name": "Coforge", "questions": [{"subtopic_id": 1, "question_text": "A train 150 meters long is traveling at a speed of 60 km/h. In how many seconds will it completely pass a person standing still on a platform?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "8 seconds", "option_b": "9 seconds", "option_c": "10 seconds", "option_d": "12 seconds", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Speed = 60 * (5/18) = 50/3 m/s. Time = Distance / Speed = 150 / (50/3) = (150 * 3) / 50 = 9 seconds.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 35, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 3, "question_text": "The ratio of savings to current account deposits in a bank branch is 3 : 5. If total combined deposits equal ₹800,000, find the total value of savings account deposits.", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "₹240,000", "option_b": "₹300,000", "option_c": "₹360,000", "option_d": "₹480,000", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Total ratio parts = 3 + 5 = 8 parts. 1 part = 800,000 / 8 = ₹100,000. Savings deposit (3 parts) = 3 * 100,000 = ₹300,000.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 2, "question_text": "A number is increased by 10% and subsequently decreased by 10%. What is the net percentage change in the number?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "0% change", "option_b": "1% increase", "option_c": "1% decrease", "option_d": "2% decrease", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "Net change = x + y + (x * y)/100 = 10 - 10 + (10 * -10)/100 = 0 - 1 = -1% (1% decrease).", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 6, "question_text": "Find the next missing number in the progression: 8, 12, 18, 26, 36, ?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "42", "option_b": "46", "option_c": "48", "option_d": "52", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "Differences between consecutive terms form an arithmetic progression: 12 - 8 = 4; 18 - 12 = 6; 26 - 18 = 8; 36 - 26 = 10. Next difference is 12. Next term = 36 + 12 = 48.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 10, "question_text": "If in a certain numerical code, CAT is coded as 24 and DOG is coded as 26, how will BAT be coded?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "21", "option_b": "22", "option_c": "23", "option_d": "25", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "Letters correspond to their alphabetical ordinal positions: C(3) + A(1) + T(20) = 24. D(4) + O(15) + G(7) = 26. BAT = B(2) + A(1) + T(20) = 23.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 14, "question_text": "What is the output printed by the following conditional pseudo code snippet?", "code_snippet": "Integer a, b, c\nSet a = 5, b = 10\nSet c = a + b * 2\nIf (c > 20) Then\n    c = c + 5\nElse\n    c = c - 5\nEnd If\nPrint c", "language": "c", "option_a": "20", "option_b": "25", "option_c": "30", "option_d": "35", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "By operator precedence, multiplication precedes addition: b * 2 = 10 * 2 = 20. Then c = 5 + 20 = 25. Condition 25 > 20 is True, so c = 25 + 5 = 30.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 21, "question_text": "What does the following recursive pseudo code algorithm compute for an input integer n >= 0?", "code_snippet": "Function Solve(int n)\n    If n == 0 or n == 1 Then\n        Return n\n    Else\n        Return Solve(n - 1) + Solve(n - 2)\n    End If\nEnd Function", "language": "c", "option_a": "Factorial of n", "option_b": "Sum of first n numbers", "option_c": "The n-th Fibonacci number", "option_d": "Power of 2 to n", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "The recurrence relation F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2) with base cases F(0)=0 and F(1)=1 defines the Fibonacci sequence.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 21, "question_text": "Which fundamental data structure is most effective for checking whether parenthesis and bracket nesting is correctly balanced in an expression string?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "Queue", "option_b": "Stack", "option_c": "Binary Search Tree", "option_d": "Array List", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "A Stack works on Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) order, allowing the most recently opened bracket to be matched against the next closing bracket.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 16, "question_text": "In Object-Oriented software engineering, the mechanism that bundles state and operations together while shielding internal implementation is called:", "code_snippet": null, "language": "java", "option_a": "Polymorphism", "option_b": "Inheritance", "option_c": "Encapsulation", "option_d": "Dynamic Dispatch", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "Encapsulation packages variables and methods together and prevents unauthorized direct tampering through controlled interfaces.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 20, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 1, "question_text": "A motorboat travels 24 km downstream in 2 hours and 16 km upstream in 2 hours. What is the speed of the water current/stream?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "1 km/h", "option_b": "2 km/h", "option_c": "3 km/h", "option_d": "4 km/h", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "Downstream speed = 24 / 2 = 12 km/h. Upstream speed = 16 / 2 = 8 km/h. Stream speed = (Downstream - Upstream) / 2 = (12 - 8) / 2 = 2 km/h.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 45, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 12, "question_text": "P can finish a job in 15 days, and Q can finish it in 10 days. With the assistance of R, they complete the entire job together in 4 days. In how many days can R complete the job alone?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "10 days", "option_b": "12 days", "option_c": "14 days", "option_d": "15 days", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "R's 1-day work = 1/4 - (1/15 + 1/10) = 1/4 - 5/30 = 1/4 - 1/6 = (3 - 2)/12 = 1/12. R working alone takes 12 days.", "difficulty": "medium", "est_time_seconds": 50, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 23, "question_text": "Statements: Some cats are dogs. All dogs are birds.\nConclusions:\nI. Some cats are birds.\nII. Some birds are dogs.\nWhich conclusion(s) logically follow?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "Only Conclusion I follows", "option_b": "Only Conclusion II follows", "option_c": "Both Conclusion I and II follow", "option_d": "Neither follows", "correct_option": "C", "explanation": "The intersection of cats and dogs is contained in birds, so some cats are birds (I follows). Since all dogs are birds, some birds are dogs (II follows).", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 35, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 8, "question_text": "Which of the following sentences utilizes the preposition of time correctly?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "She has been working on the software module since three years.", "option_b": "She has been working on the software module for three years.", "option_c": "She is working on the software module from three years.", "option_d": "She works on the software module during three years.", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "'For' is used with a period/duration of time ('three years'), whereas 'since' denotes a specific point in time ('since 2023').", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 25, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 20, "question_text": "Which standard SQL keyword is placed directly after SELECT to filter out redundant duplicate rows from the query output?", "code_snippet": null, "language": "sql", "option_a": "UNIQUE", "option_b": "DISTINCT", "option_c": "FILTER", "option_d": "GROUP", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "SELECT DISTINCT removes duplicate rows from the result set, returning only unique records.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 20, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}, {"subtopic_id": 2, "question_text": "A shopkeeper sells an electronic gadget for ₹840 and realizes a profit of 20%. What was the actual cost price of the gadget?", "code_snippet": null, "language": null, "option_a": "₹680", "option_b": "₹700", "option_c": "₹720", "option_d": "₹750", "correct_option": "B", "explanation": "SP = CP * 1.20 => 840 = CP * 1.20 => CP = 840 / 1.20 = ₹700.", "difficulty": "easy", "est_time_seconds": 30, "source_tag": "Coforge Placement PYQ"}]}]
